@page "/docs/theming/chart-colors"

@* The chart palettes: the theme's (Brand, Contrast, Okabe-Ito, Calm), choosing one for the app, part of a
   page or one chart, the per-chart palettes (Emphasis, Shades, Diverging, Of), your own colors, and
   how the palettes are checked (ChartPaletteSeparationTests, BrandChartPaletteTests). Values from
   tokens.css, ChartPalette.cs and BrandChartPalette.cs. *@

<ArticlePage Section="theming" Slug="chart-colors">
    <Lead>
        Charts, gauges and the scheduler color their series from the theme's chart palette. The default, Brand, starts
        at your brand color and keeps neighboring series apart for color-blind readers, in light and dark. You can pick
        another palette for the app, for part of a page or for one chart.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The palettes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each palette has eight colors in a fixed order. A chart's first series takes the first color, its second the
            second, and so on. The order is part of the palette: neighbors are chosen to stay apart, so a chart never skips
            or shuffles them.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Palette</th><th>Colors</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var p in Palettes)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>@p.Name</code></td>
                            <td><span style="display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; vertical-align: middle">@for (var i = 1; i <= 8; i++) { var slot = $"background: var(--ib-chart-{p.Token}{i}); width: 16px; height: 16px; border-radius: 4px; display: inline-block"; <span style="@slot"></span> }</span></td>
                            <td>@p.Use</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Brand, the default, puts the brand color first, so a chart of one series is your app's color. The rest are
            Contrast's colors without the one nearest the brand, in the order that keeps neighbors furthest apart. It
            follows <Code>Brand</Code> on the <Code>IzzyTheme</Code>, a preset's brand, and a <Code>ThemeScope</Code>
            that sets a brand of its own.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Contrast has eight colors of one lightness, each at least 3:1 on the surface in both schemes, so thin lines
            and small markers stay visible. It is the same in every app.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            With Brand or Contrast, a chart with exactly two, three or four series gets a set chosen for that count, where
            every pair stays apart, not only neighbors. Brand's sets start at the brand color; a brand that leaves no
            such set of four (teal and green) takes its first four colors. The count is the series the chart defines, so
            hiding one from the legend never repaints the others.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Okabe-Ito and Calm have some lighter colors under 3:1 in light mode. Keep a legend, labels or a table view with
            them, so no series depends on its color alone.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Choose the app's palette</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Set <Code>ChartPalette</Code> on the <Code>IzzyTheme</Code> in the <Code>head</Code>:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor">@ThemeSnippet</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            For part of a page, set it on a <Code>ThemeScope</Code>. For one chart whatever the theme's, set the chart's
            <Code>Palette</Code> to <Code>ChartPalette.Contrast</Code>, <Code>ChartPalette.OkabeIto</Code> or
            <Code>ChartPalette.Calm</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@ScopeSnippet</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Palettes for one chart</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Distinct colors say which series is which. When the series mean something more, set the chart's
            <Code>Palette</Code> to one of these.
        </TextBlock>
        <Heading Level="3">One series picked out</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ChartPalette.Emphasis(index)</Code> colors one series and grays the rest, for a chart about one of them,
            such as one store against the others. The gray keeps 3:1 on the surface, so the others still read as data.
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="chart-emphasis" />
        <Heading Level="3">Series in an order</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ChartPalette.Shades(color)</Code> runs from light to dark for series in an order, such as plan tiers.
            Past about six, shades are hard to tell apart.
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="chart-shades" />
        <Heading Level="3">Two sides of a middle</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ChartPalette.Diverging(low, high)</Code> meets at a gray middle, for answers from disagree to agree or a
            change below and above zero.
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="chart-diverging" />
        <Heading Level="3">Your own colors</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ChartPalette.Of(...)</Code> takes your colors in order, for dashboards where each entity always has the
            same color, such as a product line. A series' <Code>Color</Code> still sets that one series.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Your own palette for the app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Charts read <Code>--ib-chart-1</Code> to <Code>--ib-chart-8</Code>, and <Code>--ib-chart-of2-1</Code> to
            <Code>--ib-chart-of4-4</Code> for the two-, three- and four-series sets. Set them in your stylesheet, each as a
            <Code>light-dark()</Code> pair:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="css">@CssSnippet</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Check your colors before you ship them. Neighbors should differ for color-blind readers as well as everyone
            else, and each color should be at least 3:1 on the surface in both schemes.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">How the palettes are checked</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Every build checks the palettes in <Code>tokens.css</Code>. Distance is measured in OKLab, and color blindness
            is simulated with the Machado, Oliveira and Fernandes (2009) model. Neighbors must stay at least 8 apart for
            protanopia and deuteranopia, and 15 apart for typical vision. The two-, three- and four-series sets check every
            pair. Contrast and its sets must keep 3:1 on the surface, as must the Emphasis gray. The Brand palette is
            checked the same way for each built-in brand, and worked out with the same measure for a hex brand.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Good practice</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Keep a legend for two or more series, and label up to four series directly, so color is never the only cue.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Past eight series, fold the smallest into "Other" or split the chart into small multiples; never add colors.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Keep text in text colors. Values and labels beside a series stay in the text color; the mark carries the color.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Leave status colors (success, warning, danger) for status, not for a fourth series.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/chart">Chart</Anchor>: series, axes and the <Code>Palette</Code> parameter.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Themes and presets</Anchor>: everything else <Code>IzzyTheme</Code> sets.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/color-and-motion">Color, contrast and motion</Anchor>: contrast across the library.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record PaletteRow(string Name, string Token, string Use);

    private static readonly PaletteRow[] Palettes =
    [
        new("Brand", "", "The default. Starts at your brand color, then Contrast's colors."),
        new("Contrast", "contrast-", "The same in every app; every color stays visible on the surface."),
        new("OkabeIto", "okabe-ito-", "The widely used color-blind-safe set: seven colors, then gray. Familiar in science and reports."),
        new("Calm", "calm-", "Muted colors for dashboards people read all day."),
    ];

    private const string ThemeSnippet = """
        <IzzyTheme ChartPalette="ThemeChartPalette.OkabeIto" />
        """;

    private const string ScopeSnippet = """
        <ThemeScope ChartPalette="ThemeChartPalette.Calm">
            <Chart ...>...</Chart>
        </ThemeScope>

        <Chart Items="_sales" Palette="ChartPalette.Contrast">...</Chart>
        """;

    private const string CssSnippet = """
        :root {
          --ib-chart-1: light-dark(#1f6fd1, #4a92f0);
          --ib-chart-2: light-dark(#b8520f, #e07a3a);
          /* … to --ib-chart-8, and --ib-chart-of2-1 to --ib-chart-of4-4 */
        }
        """;
}
